Marine fire dampers are mechanical safety devices installed in ventilation ducts and air-transfer openings aboard ships. Designed to close automatically when exposed to heat, they help restrict fire propagation through ventilation systems and protect passengers, crew, cargo, and vessel infrastructure. These products are used across yachts, merchant ships, and naval vessels.

The market is benefiting from stringent maritime safety requirements and increasing investment in commercial and naval shipbuilding. Electrical actuator dampers accounted for more than 63% of the market in 2024, reflecting demand for reliable, automated responses during emergencies. Merchant ships represented over 57% of application demand, while Europe held more than 40% of the global market. Asia-Pacific is expected to record the fastest regional growth as shipbuilding activity expands in China, South Korea, and Japan.

